# Using the {{name}} SDK

## Creating a client

```zig
const sdk = @import("{{name}}");

var client = sdk.init(allocator, io, token);
defer client.deinit();
```

`sdk.init` targets `{{base_url}}`. To use a different host, call `client.withBaseUrl("https://...")`.

The token is sent as a bearer token on operations that require authentication.

## Calling operations

Every operation is a free function in `sdk.client` that takes a `*Client` first:

```zig
var result = try sdk.client.someOperation(&client, arguments);
defer result.deinit();
const value = result.value();
```

Each operation comes in three forms:

| Form | Returns | Use it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `operation` | `!Owned(T)` | You want the parsed model and treat any non-2xx status as an error. |
| `operationRaw` | `!RawResponse` | You want the status code and body. |
| `operationResult` | `!ApiResult(T)` | You want to tell API errors from parse errors. |

`Owned(T)` owns the parsed response; call `deinit()` when you are done with it.

## Error handling

`ApiResult(T)` is a union with `ok`, `api_error` (status and body) and `parse_error` (the parser error name and body) cases:

```zig
var result = try sdk.client.someOperationResult(&client, arguments);
defer result.deinit();
switch (result) {
    .ok => |owned| std.debug.print("{any}\n", .{owned.value()}),
    .api_error => |err| std.debug.print("HTTP {d}: {s}\n", .{ @intFromEnum(err.status), err.body }),
    .parse_error => |err| std.debug.print("could not parse: {s}\n", .{err.error_name}),
}
```

## Observing requests

Set `client.http_observer` to log every request, response and error.

## Known limitations

- `multipart/form-data` and `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` request bodies are sent as JSON.
- Response bodies are buffered in memory.
